#!/usr/bin/env bash
# The factory's daemon (#399), the one long-running process of the sweep: it runs the queue's pass every 60 s and takes a seat's end at once, on
# $FACTORY_STATE/daemon.sock. Only the 60 s job starts it: `bin/factory-queue sweep` starts this detached when nobody answers `ping`, with the
# job's environment and its output in ~/Library/Logs/factory/daemon.log. Nobody runs it by hand except to see it work; a second one finds the
# lock (daemon.lock) held and exits. It exits by itself after a pass that moved the checkout (the job's next run starts the new code), when its
# log passes 10 MiB, when no ping has come for 180 s, and on SIGTERM. The design, the verbs, the store and the difference from piggery
# are in the header of bin/lib/daemon.mjs; the store is $FACTORY_STATE/factory.db (bin/lib/store.mjs).
# `bin/factory-daemon linear-check --since <time> [--until <time>]` (FAC-38) starts no daemon: run as the daemon's account on its node, it reads
# Linear over the window as the Factory app, asks the running daemon which of those events its store lacks, and prints them; exit 0 none, 1 some.
# The queue's job starts the daemon with the job's PATH (bin/lib/launchd.mjs); bin/lib/prelude.sh adds the places Homebrew puts node, pi and
# codex, so the reader pass the daemon's sweep starts finds them as bin/factory-reader does.
. "$(dirname "$0")/lib/prelude.sh"
exec node "$(dirname "$0")/lib/daemon.mjs" "$@"
